    rec: [0, 0, 0, 0, 0],
    text: "**Worktree setup** (#226722): an assessment; nothing is built yet.\n\n**The \"prepare workspace\" step already exists.** Every worktree (coordinators, workers, the lander) runs the project's `isolates.worktree.setup` before its first tool call; a failure blocks tools and retries. Boxer already has one: `composer install`, `npm ci`, a fresh `.env` and Wayfinder, about 20 s.\n\n**What's missing:** setup gets no worktree id or port, nothing it sets reaches later commands, nothing runs when a worktree is removed, and every command is capped at 120 s. A fresh boxer worktree still has no database, and its dev server collides with the main checkout on :8000 and :5173.\n\n**Recommendation:** small to medium, about +150 lines, extensions only, no restart. Ultimate gives the hook, ports and environment; the database work stays in each project's own script.\n- **Ports:** each worktree reserves a block of 10 ports from 20000 up, recorded so they never collide and freed when it's removed.\n- **Environment:** setup and every command in that worktree get `ULTIMATE_PORT`, `ULTIMATE_WORKTREE`, `ULTIMATE_CONVERSATION` and `ULTIMATE_SOURCE`, plus `ULTIMATE_LANDER=1` in the lander's checkout so it can skip the database. Only ports and paths, never secrets.\n- **Teardown:** new `isolates.worktree.teardown` commands run before a finished worktree is removed, to stop its dev server and drop its database. A failure is only a notice.\n- **Timeouts:** per command, for slow database restores.\n- **Onboarding:** the `integrate-ultimate` skill gets a \"Worktree setup\" step. I read the project, write `.ultimate/worktree-setup.sh` and `-teardown.sh`, prove them in a scratch worktree and commit them. Postgres or anything with credentials becomes a question to you.\n- **Databases, in the project's script:** SQLite is a copy-on-write clone of the source file, so it's near instant with no extra disk. Postgres is a `createdb -T` from a template, dropped on teardown.\n- **For boxer:** copy the real `.env`, clone the 96 MB production SQLite file, and write `APP_URL`, `SERVER_PORT` and `VITE_PORT` from the worktree's port block.\n\n**Easier alternative:** only the onboarding step, with no core change. It can already copy `.env` and the database using `ULTIMATE_SOURCE`, but dev servers still collide on :8000, and nothing cleans up databases or servers.\n\n**Risks:** each worktree holds a copy of boxer's production data. Copy-on-write keeps disk flat and teardown deletes it, but a kept or dirty worktree keeps its copy.\n\n**Decisions** (my picks: 1a 2a 3a 4a 5a, go):\n1. **Scope:** (a) ports, environment, teardown, timeouts and the onboarding step; (b) onboarding step only; (c) (a) without teardown.\n2. **Ports:** (a) a block of 10 per worktree; (b) a single `PORT`.\n3. **Lander:** (a) `ULTIMATE_LANDER=1` so scripts skip the database there; (b) the lander runs the full script.\n4. **Onboarding:** (a) a step in the skill, proven in a scratch worktree; (b) also a setup card.\n5. **Boxer, once this lands:** (a) you tell boxer's @ultimate to read #226722 in the ultimate project (cross-project reads work) and set up its scripts; (b) you do it yourself.\n\nReply e.g. \"1a 2a 3a 4a 5a, go\".",
